For your ease of reference, we have outlined through this email a **preliminary overview of the key income tax and sales tax implications** that may arise in relation to your proposed online business model. You may kindly review the above and share your feedback or any observations you may have. In addition, we would appreciate if you could kindly clarify whether the **professional fee you referred to relates only to ensuring income tax and sales tax compliance**, or whether it is intended to **also cover accounting and bookkeeping services** for the business. This clarification will help us properly understand the scope of services expected from our side so that we may share with you a **more detailed scope of work along with a formal proposal** accordingly. In case any part of the above discussion requires further clarification, please feel free to let us know and we would be pleased to explain the matter in further detail.
:::

Please read below and take content: 

For the purpose of our views, we have been given to understand that on making such payments, the Company used to deduct tax under section 153(1)(b) before insertion of section 153B through Finance Act 2019. However, after insertion thereof the Company applied a tax withholding of 15% under section 153B, which has been omitted vide Finance Act, 2021.

3.	Royalty has been specifically defined under section 2(54) of the Ordinance, and it is our view that a payment of royalty does not constitute the provision or rendering of any service. Therefore, withholding tax under section 153(1)(b) is not applicable. Furthermore, since section 153B was omitted by the Finance Act 2021, we understand that there is currently no provision mandating tax deduction on royalty payments made to resident persons. Accordingly, no withholding tax is required on payment of Royalty to resident person(s).

4.	However, a payment should be classified as per its economic substance and the commercial activity that it represents, rather than by the nomenclature used in the documentation and agreements (i.e. substance shall prevail over the legal form in taxation). Thus, it should be evaluated whether the payment in question made to PQA is classifiable as Royalty for tax purposes in terms of the definition given under the Ordinance irrespective of how it is named in the documentation. The definition of Royalty given under section 2(54) of the Ordinance is reproduced below for quick reference:

(54) “royalty” means any amount paid or payable, however described or computed, whether periodical or a lump sum, as consideration for – 

(a)	the use of, or right to use any patent, invention, design or model, secret formula or process, trademark or other like property or right;
(b)	the use of, or right to use any copyright of a literary, artistic or scientific work, including films or video tapes for use in connection with television or tapes in connection with radio broadcasting, but shall not include consideration for the sale, distribution or exhibition of cinematograph films;
(c)	the receipt of, or right to receive, any visual images or sounds, or both, transmitted by satellite, cable, optic fibre or similar technology in connection with television, radio or internet broadcasting;
(d)	the supply of any technical, industrial, commercial or scientific knowledge, experience or skill;
(e)	the use of or right to use any industrial, commercial or scientific equipment;
(f)	the supply of any assistance that is ancillary and subsidiary to, and is furnished as a means of enabling the application or enjoyment of, any such property or right as mentioned in subclauses (a) through (e); and
(g)	the disposal of any property or right referred to in sub-clauses (a) through (e);

5.	We have been given to understand through various communications with your team from time to time that payment classified as royalty in the audited accounts is paid by QICTL to PQA based on a 'per move basis' for the volumes managed at the 1st Terminal (Berths 5 through 7) and the 2nd Terminal (Beyond Berths 5 through 7). In return, PQA undertakes responsibilities such as dredging and upkeep of the channel, turning circle, and approaches to the berth, as well as promptly facilitating vessel berthing and sailing procedures. Thus, the payment is effectively to operate and maintain the container terminal on the basis of containers / boxes handled at the Terminal maintained by QICT.

6.	We have also been given to understand that QICT has also entered into an operating lease agreement and pays rent for the use of PQA's land to achieve terminal operating business. This amount is separately shown in the audited accounts as Rent expense and is paid in terms of the Indenture of Lease which is separate from the payment of Royalty. If this is not the case then we request you to share with us the complete breakdown of payments made to PQA, to undertake a thorough analysis to accurately determine the correct classification of the benefits received by QICT in return for the subject payment.

7.	In view of the above, we understand that the subject payment to PQA, based on its economic substance, appears not to be covered within the ambit of Royalty as defined under section 2(54) of the Ordinance since it does not involve any intangible element / asset as described in the definition; rather, it pertains to the use of land and the provision of services as mentioned in para 7.
8.	Accordingly, the payment could be potentially classified as either payment of rent against the lease of land by PQA as it is understood that some portion of the total payment may pertain to the right of use of land; or it could be classified as payment for services provided by PQA to enable terminal operations. To determine that an analysis should be undertaken of the benefits received by QICT to identify the predominant and prevalent nature of benefit and consideration thereagainst. As it is substance of the transaction that matters, rather than the nomenclature and legal form.

9.	We are also aware that certain terminal operator companies are deducting tax under section 153(1)(b) on payment to port authorities. Thus, the tax authority may not accept any position for non-withholding, contending the payment to be in the nature of provision of services. Should the Company decide to not withhold tax, it would also be prudent to obtain an undertaking in writing from PQA that they would indemnify QICTL for any adverse inference drawn to it owing to such non-withholding, as otherwise the tax withheld under section 153(1)(b) would be minimum tax in the hands of PQA.

